@startuml proto-ws2812-frame
' ===========================================================================
' proto-ws2812-frame.puml - (b) the WS2812 frame and cell encoding.
'
' A WS2812 frame is 24 bits, GRB order, on a wire with no clock, no header, no
' length and no checksum. The whole "format" is:
'   * three bytes in G, R, B order (NOT RGB - the strip latches the FIRST byte
'     as green), and
'   * each bit is a PULSE, not a level: a 1 is high for most of the cell, a 0
'     is low for all of it.
'
' The frame below is the one firmware/ws2812.pe sends, 0x9C 0xE0 0x8A, and
' tb_pe_soc_ws2812.v compares the decode against these bytes written in the TB
' - not against the decode of the same run.
' ===========================================================================

title WS2812 - the 24-bit frame and the cell encoding\n<font size=12>frame 9c e0 8a, GRB, MSB first; each bit is a PULSE, not a level</font>

left to right direction
hide empty description
hide stereotype
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam rectangle {
  BorderColor #33555
  FontName monospaced
  FontSize 12
}
skinparam rectangle<<byte>> {
  BackgroundColor #F4F7FB
}
skinparam rectangle<<gate>> {
  BackgroundColor #FFF4D6
  BorderColor #C8B800
}

rectangle "RESET\n\n> 50 us of LOW\nthe pin stays DRIVEN\n\n60.85 us before\nframe 1\n62.28 us between\nframes" as RST <<gate>>

rectangle "BYTE 0 - GREEN\n\ndmem[0] = 0x9C\n1001 1100  (bit 7..0)\n\ncell  0  bit 23   1   HIGH 48\ncell  1  bit 22   0   LOW  75\ncell  2  bit 21   0   LOW  75\ncell  3  bit 20   1   HIGH 48\ncell  4  bit 19   1   HIGH 48\ncell  5  bit 18   1   HIGH 48\ncell  6  bit 17   0   LOW  75\ncell  7  bit 16   0   LOW  75\n        <b>byte boundary</b>" as B0 <<byte>>

rectangle "BYTE 1 - RED\n\ndmem[1] = 0xE0\n1110 0000  (bit 7..0)\n\ncell  8  bit 15   1   HIGH 48\ncell  9  bit 14   1   HIGH 48\ncell 10  bit 13   1   HIGH 48\ncell 11  bit 12   1   HIGH 48\ncell 12  bit 11   0   LOW  75\ncell 13  bit 10   0   LOW  75\ncell 14  bit 9    0   LOW  75\ncell 15  bit 8    0   LOW  75\n        <b>byte boundary</b>" as B1 <<byte>>

rectangle "BYTE 2 - BLUE\n\ndmem[2] = 0x8A\n1000 1010  (bit 7..0)\n\ncell 16  bit  7   1   HIGH 48\ncell 17  bit  6   0   LOW  75\ncell 18  bit  5   0   LOW  75\ncell 19  bit  4   0   LOW  75\ncell 20  bit  3   1   HIGH 48\ncell 21  bit  2   0   LOW  75\ncell 22  bit  1   0   LOW  75\ncell 23  bit  0   1   HIGH 48\n        <b>frame done</b>" as B2 <<byte>>

rectangle "RESET\n\n> 50 us of LOW\n\ndmem[7] == 2, so\nthe next cell is a\nreset, not a cell.\n\n24 x 75 clocks\n= 1 800 clocks\n= <b>30.000 us</b>" as RST2 <<gate>>

RST -[#3A7]-> B0 : cell 0
B0 -[#3A7]-> B1 : cell 8
B1 -[#3A7]-> B2 : cell 16
B2 -[#3A7]-> RST2
RST2 -[#3A7,dashed]-> RST : the frame repeats

note bottom of RST
  A single frame cannot show a
  reset AT ALL, which is why the
  firmware sends the frame twice
  and the TB anchors its second
  frame on the BETWEEN-frame
  reset - the one that matters on
  a strip that is already lit.
end note

note top of B0
  <b>Why the frame OPENS with a 1.</b>
  The cell grid is anchored on the
  frame's first RISING edge, and a
  frame whose first bit is 0 has
  none to anchor on: the line is
  already low from the reset, so the
  only evidence of where cell 0
  starts is the reset delay - a
  firmware constant the TB refuses
  to assume. The TB then ASSERTS
  cell 0 is a 1, so if the data is
  ever changed to one that opens
  with a 0, the TB says so instead
  of silently decoding a shifted
  frame.
end note

note bottom of B0
  <b>Why the byte is never shifted.</b>
  This ISA has SHR and no shift-left.
  "Mask bit 7, then shift the byte
  right" reads like MSB-first and is
  not: after k shifts the bit due at
  k sits at 7-k, so bit 7 is zero
  from the second cell on and the
  frame comes out as 24 zeros.

  Leaving the byte alone in dmem[6]
  and walking a MASK down it
  (0x80, 0x40 ... 0x01) keeps
  dmem[0..2] the frame the firmware
  was given, in the order the
  datasheet names it. A rotating
  byte leaves a rotated byte behind,
  so every later read has to know it
  has been chewed on.
end note

note bottom of B1
  <b>Why 14 instructions both ways.</b>
  The loop has two exits at the same
  point: eight bits of one byte, or
  move to the next byte. If the
  boundary cost extra instructions,
  the EIGHTH cell of every byte
  would be longer than the other
  seven and the frame would be
  malformed - a single stretched
  cell in each byte, which a
  decoder that resynchronises on
  every rising edge never notices
  and a strip does.

  So the taken path does 14
  instructions of real work, the
  not-taken path pays 14 NOPs, and
  the byte-boundary TEST is inside
  the taken path so the last byte of
  the frame does not load a fourth
  "byte" out of dmem[3] - which is
  not data.
end note

note bottom of B2
  <b>Why 0xE0 0x8A and not something else.</b>
  All three components are non-trivial
  and all three are DIFFERENT, so a
  firmware that sent RGB, GBR, BRG or
  a rotation of the frame fails the
  decode. The TB's expectation is
  bytes written in the TB, not the
  decode of the same run.
end note

legend bottom
  |= value |= high time |= low time |= cell total |=
  | <b>1</b> | 48 clocks = 800.0 ns | 27 clocks = 450.0 ns | <b>75</b> = 1.250 us |
  | <b>0</b> | 0 - low for the whole cell | 75 clocks = 1250.0 ns | <b>75</b> = 1.250 us |

  <b>48 was chosen over 50 on purpose.</b> 75 = 48 + 27 exactly, so both halves are
  the datasheet's NOMINAL figures hit to the clock. A 50-clock high gives 417 ns
  of low, 7 % under the nominal tLOW, and the receiver's margin is better spent
  matching the datasheet than maximising one side of it.

  <b>A 0 cell is low for the WHOLE 1.25 us</b> rather than carrying a 0.4 us high
  first - a deliberate simplification, measured and reported rather than left
  implied: the strip samples at about 2/3 into the cell, so a line low
  throughout is unambiguously a 0, and shaping the 0 as well would cost a second
  conditional in a cell that has none to spare.

  <b>There is no checksum and no acknowledge.</b> Nothing on the wire says the
  frame arrived. The whole of the protocol's error detection is the reset.
end legend

@enduml
